uskegon has set <strong class=\"sig\">27 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 76 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">142 record highs</strong>, far more than the 76 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (62). Muskegon's all-time high is 99\u00b0F, on July 30, 1913.","t_chg":"Muskegon has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 123 complete years: Muskegon's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.19\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.27), about +2.4\u00b0F since 1897. Muskegon's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.28\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.21 to +0.36), about +3.6\u00b0F since 1897.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 8 of 12 months. Highs rose in 9 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Muskegon has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Muskegon's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.52 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.27 to +0.77), about +6.7 inches since 1897. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 35.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 2019 (47.97 in); the driest, 1930 (16.57 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}